  *Abstract.* This paper reconstructs the development of PlanetSimulation from its Git history, source code, tests, and versioned image captures. The implementation progressed from a configurable OpenGL sphere to a terrain-bearing, hierarchical orbital scene with reflected illumination, terrain shadows, atmospheric scattering and refraction, and interactive diagnostics. We explain the numerical and rendering choices that made each stage possible: deterministic height sampling on an adaptive icosahedral mesh, absolute-time Kepler evaluation with barycentric recoil, linear-light composition, density-dependent atmospheric integration, and latency-aware quality control. Thirteen generated vector diagrams expose the algorithms; repository screenshots show actual rendering. A historical three-stage GPU benchmark is reproduced with its original hardware qualification. The final repository contains 39 automated tests, including headless OpenGL and input checks. The system remains an intentionally compact simulation: its orbital hierarchy is prescribed rather than an N-body integrator, and its 20 FPS controller is best-effort.
]
#v(0.3cm)
*Keywords:* real-time graphics · procedural terrain · Keplerian dynamics · atmospheric optics · adaptive rendering · reproducible engineering

#figure(image("figures/early/history.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Selected historical anchors. The source history contains 171 commits through `9661b8e`; the nodes group related changes rather than claiming a one-commit implementation. This figure is a generated schematic.]) <fig:history>

= Scope and method

This is an artifact study, not a claim that every rendered effect was invented here. The ordering follows `git log --reverse` from the initial commit `5a23a47` to `9661b8e`. Commit identifiers below identify implementation checkpoints; formulas are checked against the current source, and the discussion notes where a later correction changed an earlier design. Rendering images are taken from `docs/screenshots/`, whose generation manifest records commands, hashes, renderer, and UTC timestamps. Diagrams in `figures/` are explanatory reconstructions generated by `make_figures.py`; they are not captured output.

The chronology has six engineering phases: (i) configuration and a reliable graphics pipeline, (ii) planet-local terrain and navigation, (iii) hierarchical orbits and lighting, (iv) atmosphere and performance engineering, (v) interactive diagnostics, and (vi) targeted correctness fixes. @fig:history gives the sequence. The paper treats validation as part of each phase: visual plausibility alone cannot establish a matrix convention, preserve a stable surface during walking, or prove that a shadowed ocean receives no direct reflection.

= 9–13 September: a testable rendering foundation

== From configuration to a projected sphere

The initial build and math abstractions (`1385b74`) were followed by structured JSON scenario parsing (`bd73ba9`) and a GLFW/OpenGL loop (`c96f609`). The first visible output was a camera-framed Sun and planet (`b87d5af`), then a shader/mesh pipeline (`8246a31`). A position in a planet's local mesh is transformed by a model matrix, a camera view matrix, and a projection matrix. In the project's eventual column-vector convention,

$ x_"clip" = P V M x_"local", quad x_"ndc" = x_"clip" / w_"clip" . $

This equation was operationally significant: the early history contains repeated fixes to cross-product order, handedness, matrix storage, and multiplication indices. Tests eventually asserted behavior rather than literal matrix entries; on 15 September, `ee48919` replaced the custom `Matrix4` and `Vector3` implementation with GLM. That reduced the number of independent conventions the project had to maintain. @fig:transform records the resulting data path.

#figure(image("figures/early/transforms.svg", width: 100%), caption: [The foundational transform and shading path. `P V M` follows the current shader convention; the final display transform happens after linear-light composition. Generated schematic.]) <fig:transform>

A hidden-window `--render-test` and PNG smoke test appeared in `94b42f6`. That made camera framing, framebuffer readback, and image output observable in CI-like environments. The writer evolved through several early PNG attempts and now resides in `src/rendering/diagnostics/PngWriter.h`, using libpng. @fig:solar is an actual headless capture of the compact regression scene. It should be read as evidence of the current renderer at a fixed scene, not as a screenshot of the 2026-09-11 binary.

#figure(image("../screenshots/solar-view.png", width: 82%), caption: [Current headless rendering of the frozen compact scenario. It preserves the early scientific question—are the bodies positioned and projected correctly?—while using the present version's renderer. Source: `docs/screenshots/solar-view.png`; provenance: `docs/captures/generation.json`.]) <fig:solar>

== A reproducible scene as an experimental unit

The JSON parser performs type and range checks before GPU resources are replaced. Later scene replay stores a resolved scenario, camera, time, and quality choices next to a PNG. This is more useful than a screenshot alone: the image is an observation, while the sidecar specifies a repeatable input state. On live reload, a new CPU scene is staged before the current one is exchanged, so an invalid or half-written file retains the last valid scene. The archive therefore separates three kinds of evidence: source history, executable tests, and image captures with replay metadata.

= 14–16 September: terrain and navigation on a rotating sphere

== Procedural surface as a camera-independent function

The 15 September sequence `1f8a07a` through `3d73170` introduced and refined noise-driven planet generation. The height function is evaluated in a normalized planet-local radial direction $u$, not in camera coordinates. A simplified form is

$ h(u) = h_"landscape"(u) + sum_i A_i f_i(u; "seed"_i, "frequency"_i, "octaves"_i). $

The implemented functions include value-noise fBm and a ridged variant. Broad continent and cliff fields determine large-scale shape; finer octaves add local detail. The seed and local coordinate frame make repeated samples of the same point deterministic. This matters because terrain geometry is rebuilt as the camera moves: if $h$ changed with view distance, the ground itself would rise and fall. The current code explicitly samples `heightAt(radial)` during every LOD rebuild. @fig:terrain distinguishes the fixed field from its variable triangulation.

#figure(image("figures/early/terrain.svg", width: 100%), caption: [The height field is fixed in planet coordinates; near, middle, and far zones change only sampling density. Bars are illustrative edge densities, not an image of the actual mesh. Generated schematic.]) <fig:terrain>

The sphere begins with 20 icosahedral faces, subdivided twice into 320 stable base faces. For nearby views, each base face is assigned one of three edge-segment zones using its angular distance to the eye. A previous-zone hysteresis margin prevents repeated switching at a threshold. Steep faces can receive extra segments if the triangle budget permits. Edges shared by unequal zones are sampled once at the finer requirement, then reused on both sides; concentric interior rings fill the faces without T-junctions. The budget loop first removes optional steep refinement and then lowers less-important zones. This is an engineering compromise between stable walking geometry, crack-free transitions, and a hard per-planet triangle ceiling.

The sampled height feeds normals and terrain color factors as well as eye clearance. Water is a separate spherical shell. The local camera follows the planet's translation and rotation through a planet-fixed frame, so the same latitude/longitude refers to the same terrain location after orbital motion or spin. @fig:surface shows the current rendered outcome rather than an intermediate development snapshot.

#figure(image("../screenshots/surface-view.png", width: 83%), caption: [Current surface-camera capture of procedurally generated terrain. The geometry derives from the stable height function while its tessellation is selected for this camera. Source: current gallery.]) <fig:surface>

= 24 September: absolute-time dynamics and illumination

== Prescribed Kepler ellipses with collective mass

The major simulation change `18ffbad` replaced decorative orbital speed metadata with a hierarchy of Kepler ellipses. A body names its parent and supplies semi-major and semi-minor axes $a,b$, a mean anomaly, inclination, ascending node, and periapsis orientation. Eccentricity and period follow

$ e = sqrt(1 - b^2 / a^2), quad mu = G (m_"parent" + m_"collective"), quad T = 2 pi sqrt(a^3 / mu). $

Here $a$ and $b$ are converted from the scenario's distance unit to metres before applying the SI gravitational constant; the code performs the equivalent calculation in world units. At time $t$, mean anomaly advances linearly. A bounded bracketing solve obtains eccentric anomaly $E$ from $M = E - e sin E$, and the orbital-plane position is $(a(cos E-e), b sin E)$. Three rotations orient that ellipse in the world. The current implementation uses a rationalized form near extreme periapsis to avoid cancellation. Evaluating at absolute time, rather than integrating velocity frame by frame, avoids cumulative drift and makes pausing, replay, and speed changes deterministic.

#figure(image("figures/dynamics/kepler.svg", width: 100%), caption: [An ellipse is specified relative to a focus; a child subtree and its parent recoil around a mass-weighted center. The right panel abstracts the Earth–Moon relation; sizes and offsets are not to scale. Generated schematic.]) <fig:kepler>

The hierarchy is not a full N-body solver. Each subtree acts as a collective point mass in its parent's prescribed ellipse; children induce mass-weighted recoil of the parent. Thus an Earth–Moon pair has an internal barycenter, and the Sun moves in response to its descendants while its configured position remains the time-zero anchor. The invariants to test are mass-weighted center of mass, finite positions at high eccentricity, and phase consistency across different frame cadences. Inter-branch perturbations and tidal evolution remain outside the model.

For an `O` overlay added later, the application samples the complete hierarchy at 321 times for each body: ten revolutions at 32 segments per revolution. A moon's inertial trail must therefore inherit its parent's translation rather than drawing one stationary local ellipse. @fig:trails previews this later visualization while making the distinction explicit.

#figure(image("figures/dynamics/orbit-trails.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Illustrative future moon trail in the inertial world frame. The repeated local cycles travel with the parent; the curve is explanatory and does not plot a measured scenario trajectory. Generated schematic.]) <fig:trails>

== Light transport before atmospheric composition

`01c0b70` introduced moonlight; `96e2014` added terrain shadows; `379544a` connected exposure to scene illumination. Per-frame CPU lighting computes direct flux with inverse-square distance and reflected flux with geometric albedo, a diffuse-sphere phase function, and inverse-square reflector-to-receiver falloff. Sun luminosity is derived from absolute bolometric magnitude using the IAU zero point. Shaders sum direct, reflected, and indirect contributions in linear RGB before an exponential display shoulder and sRGB encoding. This ordering is essential: summing display-encoded values would change the physical meaning of each source.

A directional orthographic depth map is made per planet from the local Sun direction. The same map can constrain opaque terrain, water, and later direct atmospheric scattering. The map is cached until local Sun direction, terrain revision, extent, or resolution changes enough to alter its result. @fig:lighting separates the direct and reflected paths and the visibility query.

#figure(image("figures/dynamics/lighting.svg", width: 100%), caption: [The Sun contributes direct flux to each body, the Moon reflects a phase-dependent portion toward the planet, and terrain visibility controls direct light. The diagram is schematic; arrow widths do not encode flux.]) <fig:lighting>

#figure(grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr), gutter: 8pt,
