= 26 September: atmospheric optics and performance engineering

== Optical state and curved view rays

`0e48155` added the atmosphere's composition, density, scattering, and scene composition. For altitude $h$, the two optical populations use exponential profiles, schematically $rho_"m"(h) = exp(-h/H_"m")$ for molecules and $rho_"a"(h) = exp(-h/H_"a")$ for aerosols. Pressure, temperature, and gas composition determine an approximate visible-band refractivity and Rayleigh coefficients; dust and condensed water add aerosol extinction. The RGB single-scattering source at a view-path sample combines incoming sunlight, phase functions, local density, and transmittance. The direct term now also queries terrain shadow visibility. This is a compact runtime approximation to the scattering formulation in Bruneton and Neyret's work (R2), not their full precomputed multiple-scattering solution.

The configured `temperature_k` enters the reference air state. At fixed pressure, lower temperature raises density and the modeled refractive index. A midpoint integrator bends a view ray according to the perpendicular refractive-index gradient,

$ (d vec(d)) / (d s) = (nabla n - vec(d) (vec(d) · nabla n)) / n. $

The shell index tapers continuously to vacuum at its boundary. CPU tests check ray reciprocity and the spherical Snell invariant $n r sin theta$; GPU tests compare reprojected sky displacement with the CPU reference. The later commit `9661b8e` added direct cold-versus-warm JSON regressions. @fig:refraction separates the geometric ray from atmospheric contribution.

#figure(image("figures/atmosphere/refraction.svg", width: 82%), caption: [A refractive view ray bends through denser air while solar light contributes scattered radiance. Near terrain keeps its rasterized silhouette; only visible distant imagery is reprojected. Generated schematic.]) <fig:refraction>

The current implementation does not bend terrain geometry or direct-light shadow rays. A curved ray that escapes the shell may reproject a visible distant image pixel; an off-screen or hidden source cannot be reconstructed. Trapped rays do not leak background light. These choices preserve depth and body identity near opaque surfaces while limiting the integration cost. The underlying refractive-index dependence on atmospheric state is well established in metrology (R3), but the constants and miniature-world scale here are an artistic rendering model, not a precision terrestrial atmosphere.

