== Reducing atmospheric cost without changing its data contract

The first atmosphere was visibly correct but expensive. `fa1a294` added a reusable GPU density-column table, reduced-resolution scattering fields, depth-aware resolve, paused-frame reuse, and GPU pass profiling. Shell-dependent columns need not be regenerated for camera motion or a changed gas mixture. The main atmosphere field normally uses quarter resolution; when reduced samples do not match full-resolution depth, the pixel is integrated at full resolution. Terrain, stars, and the Sun remain full resolution. The resulting pipeline is summarized in @fig:atmos-pipeline.

#figure(image("figures/atmosphere/pipeline.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Atmospheric rendering graph. A reusable density-column lookup and a reduced scattering field lower repeated work; full-resolution depth controls edge reconstruction. Generated schematic.]) <fig:atmos-pipeline>

The project's recorded Quadro M1000M benchmark at 1280×720 reported 208 ms mean frame time before optimization, 138 ms after the column lookup, and 26 ms after reduced fields and shared ray calculations. These were 90-frame measurements of one surface scene with Debug CPU builds, recorded in the README; @fig:perf reproduces those historical values rather than claiming a new experiment. The approximately eightfold change is specific to that workload and GPU. A later RelWithDebInfo run reported 28.5 ms. A separate headless software-rendered capture is not comparable to this hardware benchmark.

#figure(image("figures/atmosphere/performance.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Historical mean frame times from `README.md`, Quadro M1000M, 1280×720, 90 frames. These bars are a visualization of previously reported measurements, not a new benchmark.]) <fig:perf>

A profiler now records CPU stage times and asynchronous GPU timestamp results without blocking for each query. CPU frame time, GPU pass time, and swap/presentation delay answer different questions; adding CPU and GPU times would double-count overlap. A completed HDR scene may be reused if camera, time, viewport, geometry, and pending-terrain state match. The HUD remains separately drawn so input can reveal or hide it immediately.

= 28 September: observability, quality control and correctness

== Diagnostics, dependency boundaries and adaptive quality

The diagnostics work `ab59069` added optional Linux NVIDIA NVML utilization to the existing `I`/Tab panel and organized source, shaders, tests, screenshots, and replay metadata by subsystem. Utilization is sampled off the render thread and is device-wide, while GPU pass milliseconds belong to the application's measured passes. Software rendering and ambiguous GPU matching show `N/A`; a fake driver library tests the ABI path without claiming validation on NVIDIA hardware. @fig:overlay is the real captured panel.

#figure(image("../screenshots/performance-overlay.png", width: 83%), caption: [Actual current performance overlay under Xvfb/Mesa llvmpipe. The white-bordered black panel reports FPS, frame time, GPU pass time, and utilization availability; its numbers are capture-environment observations.]) <fig:overlay>

`5c08479` implemented a best-effort 20 FPS quality target. A smoothed frame estimate $m_t = 0.85 m_(t-1) + 0.15 f_t$ selects one of six scene-resolution scales, from 100% down to 25%. After a 1.5-second cooldown, sustained times above 55 ms lower resolution; times below 32 ms permit recovery. Only moving interactive scenes feed the controller. A graphics-memory estimate can cap the initial scale, reserving most reported memory for other uses; fixed-resolution captures remain deterministic. The HUD stays at native window resolution. @fig:adaptive shows the control law, not a measured response trace. A strict 20 FPS guarantee is impossible once the minimum scale is reached or scene cost is unrelated to pixel count.

#figure(image("figures/interaction/adaptive.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Actual controller thresholds and discrete scene scales; bar heights are relative scale, not measured frame rates. Generated schematic.]) <fig:adaptive>

== Geometric and illumination regressions

The later fixes address failures that a plausible screenshot could conceal. `1eafe2b` corrected polar walking: numerically carrying a latitude/longitude heading through a pole reverses the local north/east basis, making the camera spin. The new walk step moves on a great circle and parallel-transports view and up about the axis $u × t$ by angle $"distance"/"radius"$. The transported basis is then expressed in the new tangent frame. @fig:pole illustrates the singularity avoided by this construction.

#figure(image("figures/early/pole.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Great-circle position update and basis transport across the north pole. At the pole, a fresh longitude-derived frame can flip; a transported 3-D basis remains continuous. Generated schematic.]) <fig:pole>

`47394a7` reused each planet's terrain depth map inside direct atmospheric view-path scattering, so a mountain can block sunlight from nearby air. The finite solar disk's twilight region is retained, and indirect atmospheric fill remains. `73ce15f` addressed water on a planet's shadow side: a valid reflection texture coordinate alone is insufficient evidence of sunlight. The reflection term is gated by local positive Sun incidence and terrain visibility, while indirect water radiance remains. @fig:ocean illustrates the separation. The gallery's terrain-shadow and twilight cases are executable visual regressions rather than uncalibrated beauty shots.

#figure(image("figures/dynamics/ocean.svg", width: 100%), caption: [A reflected bright sample can exist in image space even when the water point lies on the night side. Local incidence and shadow-map visibility gate the direct reflection term. Generated schematic.]) <fig:ocean>

