== Explanatory overlays and temporal camera continuity

`8cd59ef` introduced ten-revolution predictive trails and body cards. The path color averages terrain vertex RGB factors multiplied by the body's configured base tint; labels report name, parent, axes, eccentricity, period, radius, and mass. Sampling the entire orbital system, rather than each child's local ellipse alone, is necessary for moving-parent trails. The overlay is toggled by `O` and hidden in planet and surface cameras; real X11 input tests verify both behaviors.

Finally, `3e87f68` made orbit-to-surface entry a one-second wall-clock transition. The starting pose is the last actually rendered view, avoiding a first-frame jump after the key event. An eased parameter $q(u)=3u^2-2u^3$ drives spherical interpolation of the planet-relative radial direction, logarithmic interpolation of radius, quaternion interpolation of orientation, and field-of-view interpolation. Intermediate positions are clamped above sampled terrain and water. The target is resampled while the planet moves, so the transition ends at the current surface pose rather than a stale one. Surface walking and cursor capture begin after the descent; switching modes cancels it. @fig:transition shows the path and ease curve.

#figure(image("figures/interaction/transition.svg", width: 100%), caption: [Orbit-to-surface descent. The curve shows the implemented smoothstep parameter; the spatial path is schematic because the actual start, target, and terrain vary by scene.]) <fig:transition>

= Evaluation, limitations and reproducibility

== What the evidence establishes

The final tree at `9661b8e` contains 39 CTest entries. The suite covers configuration and orbital invariants; terrain and camera behavior; lighting, shadows, atmosphere and refraction on OpenGL; renderer captures; adaptive quality; and real GLFW input under Xvfb. In the development environment, all 39 passed under Mesa llvmpipe. The 12 README images were regenerated for version 0.0.1 and checked against their SHA-256 manifest. These facts establish that the specified examples and regressions work on the tested software renderer. They do not establish physical fidelity, portability to every GPU driver, or a guaranteed 20 FPS for arbitrary scenes.

The main model boundaries are deliberate. Orbits are prescribed two-body ellipses with hierarchical recoil, not numerically integrated mutual gravitation. Terrain is a deterministic synthetic height field with bounded tessellation; it is not geophysical erosion. Refraction bends view rays and reprojects available distant imagery but keeps near opaque geometry and direct shadow rays straight. Atmospheric temperature is read from JSON rather than evolved from sunlight and orbit. Device utilization is unavailable without a compatible, unambiguous NVIDIA driver. These constraints are important when interpreting visual results.

== Rebuilding the paper and the evidence

From the repository root, regenerate the deterministic vector figures and compile the Typst source:

```sh
python3 docs/journal/make_figures.py
typst compile --root . --creation-timestamp 1790607816 docs/journal/paper.typ docs/journal/paper.pdf
```

The 13 SVGs require only Python's standard library. The fixed PDF creation timestamp is the final implementation commit's timestamp, so repeated builds do not encode the compilation time. The paper references the versioned PNGs already stored in `docs/screenshots/`. To rerun the application tests in a headless OpenGL environment, build the CMake project with testing enabled and execute `LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 xvfb-run -a ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure`. Exact image-generation commands and checksums are in `docs/captures/generation.json`.

= Conclusion

The chronological record shows a recurring pattern: a visual capability entered the renderer, a representation or performance boundary became visible, and a testable correction clarified that boundary. The result is a compact but coupled planetary system in which geometry, motion, lighting, atmosphere, quality control, and camera semantics are coordinated by explicit data contracts. The most transferable techniques are the separation of a stable sampled surface from its adaptive mesh, absolute-time evaluation of hierarchical orbits, linear-light reuse of visibility information, and nonblocking instrumentation that informs rendering decisions without becoming the workload being measured.

